CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ strong_ordering: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ strong_ordering, equal: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ weak_ordering: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ partial_ordering: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ reference operands: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
`M <=> M' (no operator<=>) became the C int lowering, so the defaulted
operator<=> returned an int.  Without that lowering the comparison is
synthesized from == and <; an int& operand of <=> is dereferenced.
